1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,381
This is MATH 239, video 5 b. The topic is combinatorial probability, from Sections 1.1.7 and 1.2 of the notes.

2
00:00:09,831 --> 00:00:21,069
After this video, you can find a probability by counting two sets. You can count the complement when the event is hard to count. You can explain why the model changes the answer. And you can count poker hands.

3
00:00:21,519 --> 00:00:39,306
We add one assumption to counting: every possible outcome is equally likely. Let omega be the set of all outcomes. An event E is a subset of omega. Each outcome has probability 1 over the size of omega. So the probability of E is the size of E over the size of omega.

4
00:00:39,756 --> 00:00:50,669
Example 1.16. Choose a random subset of 1 to 8. What is the probability that it has at most 3 elements? The bars show how many subsets have each size k.

5
00:00:51,119 --> 00:01:06,816
An outcome is a subset, so omega has 2 to the 8, which is 256, elements. The event E is the subsets of size 0, 1, 2 or 3. By the OR rule, add 1, 8, 28 and 56. That is 93.

6
00:01:07,266 --> 00:01:13,706
So the probability is 93 over 256, which is about 0.363.

7
00:01:14,156 --> 00:01:26,138
Example 1.17. Choose a random list of the six letters a to f. What is the probability that the letters f, a, d appear together, in this order? Pause the video and try it.

8
00:01:26,588 --> 00:01:41,866
In such a list, f a d is one block. Treat the block as one new element. Then the list is a list of the four elements b, c, e and the block f a d. Each list with the block gives exactly one such list, and back.

9
00:01:42,316 --> 00:01:56,388
There are 4 factorial, which is 24, lists of four elements. There are 6 factorial, which is 720, lists in all. So the probability is 24 over 720, which is 1 over 30.

10
00:01:56,838 --> 00:02:05,522
Example 1.18. Choose a random multiset of size 2 with t types. What is the probability that both elements have the same type?

11
00:02:05,972 --> 00:02:24,594
By the multiset count from video 2, there are 2 plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1 outcomes. This equals t plus 1 choose 2, which is t plus 1 times t over 2. For t equal to 4, the grid shows the 10 multisets. The blue cells have two different types.

12
00:02:25,044 --> 00:02:31,639
The event has t outcomes: choose one type and take two elements of it. These are the green cells on the diagonal.

13
00:02:32,089 --> 00:02:46,439
So the probability is t over t plus 1 times t over 2, which is 2 over t plus 1. For t equal to 4, it is 4 over 10, or 0.4. As t grows, the probability goes to 0.

14
00:02:46,889 --> 00:03:00,078
Exercise 1.13 asks about a random permutation of 1 to 7. There are 5040 permutations. Part 1: what is the probability that 1 and 2 are next to each other? Pause the video and try it.

15
00:03:00,528 --> 00:03:15,657
Use a block again. The block holds 1 and 2 in one of 2 orders. Then we list 6 elements: the block and the other five numbers. By the AND rule, there are 2 times 6 factorial, which is 1440, permutations.

16
00:03:16,107 --> 00:03:20,797
So the probability is 1440 over 5040, which is 2 over 7.

17
00:03:21,247 --> 00:03:34,064
Part 2: what is the probability that 1 is to the left of 2? Swap the numbers 1 and 2 in a permutation. A permutation with 1 on the left goes to one with 2 on the left. Swap again, and you are back.

18
00:03:34,514 --> 00:03:42,734
So the swap is a bijection between the two halves. Each half has 2520 permutations, and the probability is 1 over 2.

19
00:03:43,184 --> 00:03:59,158
Part 3: what is the probability that no two odd numbers are next to each other? Pause the video and try it. There are four odd numbers and three even numbers. Four odd numbers need a gap between each pair. In 7 places, the only choice is places 1, 3, 5 and 7.

20
00:03:59,608 --> 00:04:06,806
So the pattern is fixed: odd, even, odd, even, odd, even, odd. Here is one permutation with this pattern.

21
00:04:07,256 --> 00:04:23,417
The odd numbers fill their four places in 4 factorial orders. The even numbers fill their three places in 3 factorial orders. By the AND rule, there are 144 permutations, and the probability is 144 over 5040, which is 1 over 35.

22
00:04:23,867 --> 00:04:45,230
Example 1.20. Let p of n be the probability that at least two of n random people have the same birthday. The notes use 365 days and assume that birthdays are uniform and independent. p of 1 is 0. If n is more than 365, p of n is 1, because two people must share a day.

23
00:04:45,680 --> 00:05:09,318
For other n, at least two equal birthdays is hard to count directly. The complement is easy: all n birthdays are different. List the people P 1 to P n. An outcome is the list of their birthdays. Try a small case first: a year of 4 days and 3 people. Each person has 4 choices, so there are 64 lists.

24
00:05:09,768 --> 00:05:18,545
All birthdays different: the first person has 4 choices, the second 3, the third 2. This is a partial list of the 4 days, so there are 24.

25
00:05:18,995 --> 00:05:30,465
So the probability of a common birthday is 1 minus 24 over 64, which is 40 over 64, or 5 over 8. A list of all 64 outcomes by computer confirms 40.

26
00:05:30,915 --> 00:05:54,507
With 365 days, the lists with all birthdays different are the partial lists of length n of the 365 days. There are 365 factorial over 365 minus n factorial of them. All lists number 365 to the n. So 1 minus p of n is the ratio, and p of n is 1 minus the ratio.

27
00:05:54,957 --> 00:06:06,799
The blue curve is p of n for n from 1 to 80. It rises fast. At n equal to 30, it is already about 0.71. At n equal to 50, it is about 0.97.

28
00:06:07,249 --> 00:06:20,438
The orange point is n equal to 23. There, p of 23 is 0.507297, just above one half. So in a group of 23 people, a shared birthday is slightly more likely than not.

29
00:06:20,888 --> 00:06:32,823
Example 1.21 shows that a probability depends on the model. A packet of Wine Gums has 10 candies. Each candy has one of five flavours. What is the probability of exactly k purple candies?

30
00:06:33,273 --> 00:06:45,348
In the dice model, we record the order of the candies in the roll, from bottom to top. These two rolls are different outcomes. Each candy has 5 choices, so there are 5 to the 10 outcomes, nearly 10 million.

31
00:06:45,798 --> 00:07:07,206
In the multiset model, only the number of candies of each flavour matters. Both rolls give the same multiset, 2, 3, 2, 2, 1. A multiset of size 10 with 5 types: there are 14 choose 4, which is 1001, outcomes. Each model makes its own outcomes equally likely.

32
00:07:07,656 --> 00:07:15,876
First the dice model. Fix k, for example k equal to 2. Choose the positions of the purple candies. There are 10 choose k ways.

33
00:07:16,326 --> 00:07:25,382
Each of the other 10 minus k candies has one of the 4 other flavours. So there are 4 to the 10 minus k ways for them. By the AND rule, multiply.

34
00:07:25,832 --> 00:07:38,510
Divide by all 5 to the 10 outcomes. For k equal to 2: 45 times 4 to the 8, over 5 to the 10. That is 0.301990.

35
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:46,205
Now the multiset model. If k candies are purple, the other 10 minus k candies form a multiset with the 4 other flavours.

36
00:07:46,655 --> 00:07:57,754
Their numbers m G, m Y, m O and m R add to 10 minus k. By the multiset count, there are 13 minus k choose 3 such multisets.

37
00:07:58,204 --> 00:08:16,050
Divide by 1001. For k equal to 2, there are 11 choose 3, which is 165, packets. So m of 2 is 165 over 1001, which is 0.164835. This is about half of d of 2.

38
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:23,140
Here are both models side by side. In the dice model, the blue bars, 2 purple candies is the most likely result.

39
00:08:23,590 --> 00:08:36,779
In the multiset model, the orange bars, 0 purple candies is the most likely result, and the bars fall steadily. The two models give different answers. Only measurements of real packets can tell which model is closer to the truth.

40
00:08:37,229 --> 00:08:57,245
Why are they different? One multiset comes from many sequences, and the number changes with the multiset. This number is the multinomial coefficient, n factorial over the product of the m i factorials. For 2, 3, 2, 2, 1 it is 75600. A multiset with all 10 candies purple comes from only 1 sequence.

41
00:08:57,695 --> 00:09:15,992
Section 1.2.4 counts poker hands. The deck has 52 cards. Each row is a suit and each column is a value. A hand is any 5 cards. The order does not matter, so a hand is a 5 element subset of the deck. The orange cells are one hand: one pair of queens.

42
00:09:16,442 --> 00:09:22,619
Practice problem P1-2 asks: how many distinct poker hands are there? Pause the video and try it.

43
00:09:23,069 --> 00:09:38,394
A hand is a 5 element subset of a 52 element set. So there are 52 choose 5, which is 2,598,960, hands. For the hand types, remember one rule: an Ace can be below 2 or above King.

44
00:09:38,844 --> 00:09:49,804
Example 1.22 counts two types. First the Straight: five consecutive values, but not all of the same suit. The orange hand is 8, 9, 10, Jack, Queen.

45
00:09:50,254 --> 00:10:02,560
Choose the values. The lowest value can be Ace, 2, up to 10. The last run is 10, Jack, Queen, King, Ace. So there are 10 choices.

46
00:10:03,010 --> 00:10:14,249
AND choose a suit for each of the five cards: 4 to the 5 choices. But 4 of these choices give all five cards one suit. That is a Straight Flush, so remove these 4.

47
00:10:14,699 --> 00:10:19,807
So there are 10 times 1020, which is 10,200, Straights.

48
00:10:20,257 --> 00:10:29,638
Next, the Busted hand: any hand that is not of a better type. So it has no pair, no straight and no flush. The orange hand is one example.

49
00:10:30,088 --> 00:10:42,440
No pair means five different values: 13 choose 5, which is 1287, sets. No straight means the values are not one of the 10 runs. So 1277 value sets remain.

50
00:10:42,890 --> 00:10:49,763
No flush means the suits are not all equal: 4 to the 5 minus 4, which is 1020, choices.

51
00:10:50,213 --> 00:11:01,451
By the AND rule, there are 1277 times 1020, which is 1,302,540, Busted hands. This is about half of all hands.

52
00:11:01,901 --> 00:11:16,065
Exercise 1.14 asks for the other rows of the table. Try two of them: Full House and Two Pair. A Full House has three cards of one value and two cards of another value. Pause the video and try it.

53
00:11:16,515 --> 00:11:26,593
Choose the value of the three cards: 13 ways. Choose 3 of its 4 suits. Then choose the value of the pair from the other 12 values, and 2 of its 4 suits.

54
00:11:27,043 --> 00:11:35,356
By the AND rule, 13 times 4 times 12 times 6 is 3744. The orange hand is three 9s and two Aces.

55
00:11:35,806 --> 00:11:50,527
Two Pair: choose the two pair values as a set, 13 choose 2. Do not choose them in order, because the two pairs play the same role. Choose 2 suits for each pair. Then the fifth card has one of the 11 other values, so 44 cards are possible.

56
00:11:50,977 --> 00:11:59,454
13 choose 2 is 78. 78 times 6 times 6 times 44 is 123,552.

57
00:11:59,904 --> 00:12:17,180
Here is the table from the notes. N is the number of hands of each type, and p is N over 52 choose 5. The green rows are the ones we counted. Each hand has exactly one type, so by the OR rule the rows add to 2,598,960.

58
00:12:17,630 --> 00:12:32,026
The other rows use the same method. For example, One Pair is 13 times 4 choose 2, times 12 choose 3 for the three other values, times 4 cubed for their suits. A computer list of every hand gives all nine counts of the table.

59
00:12:32,476 --> 00:12:43,018
To recap. With equally likely outcomes, a probability is the size of the event over the size of omega. A block of letters or numbers becomes one element.

60
00:12:43,468 --> 00:12:52,477
When the event is hard to count, count the complement, as in the birthday problem. And the model decides what an outcome is, as the Wine Gums show.

61
00:12:52,927 --> 00:13:01,468
Poker hands use the AND rule for values and suits, and subtract the cases of a better type. The next video covers the Binomial Theorem and the binomial series.
